          %h2#nonstandard_analysis Nonstandard analysis
          %p
            If the point is to do with infinitesimal numbers everything that we can do with ordinary numbers, then why not use high-powered
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
            to do this for us? That is the approach taken by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Abraham+Robinson"} Abraham Robinson
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonstandard+analysis"} nonstandard analysis
          %p
            Nonstandard analysis can be applied to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            as a whole, so it treats complex numbers, cardinal numbers, and the rest all together.
          %p …
          %h2#smooth_toposes Smooth toposes
          %p
            Another approach is to focus only on what we want to do with infinitesimals in a particular field. Since infinitesimals were used to do calculus, then let's just do calculus. We can take as axiomatic the familiar properties of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+map"} smooth functions
            used in calculus, including the ways these were applied to infinitesimals before analysis was made rigorous (by modern standards) in the 19th century. This is the approach taken by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bill+Lawvere"} Bill Lawvere
            and others in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetic differential geometry
          %p …
          %h2#other_approaches Other approaches
          %p
            The
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surreal+number"} surreal number
            = succeed "." do
